  <div class="story">
    <div><h3>The problem</h3><p>The old site ran on WordPress under the company’s previous name, with thousands of indexed pages and a design that looked like any other analytics vendor. The product had grown into a full creator-marketing lifecycle, and the site did not explain it.</p><p>The team also needed to keep editing copy every week without a developer in the loop, and without breaking a design they had just paid for.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What I built</h3><p>I designed a new visual language around a printed circuit board: navy substrate, copper-like traces, red signal pulses, white “paper” bands for reading. Then I built every page in it, including the lifecycle orbital, the animated data cards and the coded product mock-ups.</p><p>The site compiles to static HTML, deploys through GitHub Actions to a staging and a production environment, and reads its blog from a WordPress snapshot committed to the repository.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What made it hard</h3><p>Dropping the server meant every redirect, the staging password and the trailing-slash routing had to fit in one edge function with a 10 KB limit and an old JavaScript dialect. I audited 2,935 legacy URLs and collapsed whole groups into prefix rules to make the table fit.</p><p>The second hard part was people. Non-technical editors now change the site through an AI assistant, so I wrote the commands, permissions and a design lint that keep those changes inside the system.</p></div>
  </div>

