  <div class="block-title"><h3>Under the floor</h3><span class="label">How it works</span></div>
  <div class="eng">
    <div class="eng-diagram">
      <div class="lanes">
        <div class="lane"><span class="ln">Content</span>
          <div class="nd"><b>WordPress</b><i>blog and reports</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Sync script</b><i>sync-wordpress.mjs</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Committed snapshot</b><i>JSON + images in git</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Image optimiser</b><i>WebP at fixed widths</i></div>
        </div>
        <div class="jn"><i></i><span>Committed files are the only input the build reads</span></div>
        <div class="lane"><span class="ln">Build</span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Git push</b><i>staging or main</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>GitHub Actions</b><i>type, lint, design, format</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd hot"><b>Static export</b><i>next build → HTML</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Storage bucket</b><i>one per environment</i></div>
        </div>
        <div class="jn"><i></i><span>Deploy syncs the bucket, then clears the CDN cache</span></div>
        <div class="lane"><span class="ln">Request</span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Visitor</b><i>old or new URL</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd hot"><b>Edge function</b><i>auth · 301 · rewrite</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>CDN cache</b><i>CloudFront</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>HTML file</b><i>/pricing/index.html</i></div>
        </div>
        <div class="jn"><i></i><span>The page arrives complete; scripts are extras</span></div>
        <div class="lane"><span class="ln">Browser</span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Consent banner</b><i>default: denied</i></div><span class="wr"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Analytics</b><i>loads after accept</i></div><span class="wr off"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Demo form</b><i>posts to the CRM</i></div><span class="wr off"></span>
          <div class="nd"><b>Hero video</b><i>Mux, poster first</i></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="eng-notes">
      <div><h4>Redirects that fit in 10 KB</h4><p>CloudFront Functions allow 10 KB of source, about one millisecond of CPU, and only the 2009 version of JavaScript. Listing every old address would not fit, so whole groups became prefix rules. One rule covers all 27 old report URLs. A test file parses the function in the old dialect and runs 24 cases before it is uploaded.</p></div>
      <div><h4>No image server</h4><p>A static export cannot resize images on request. A script generates WebP versions at fixed widths during the build and a custom loader points each image at the right file. The hero poster dropped from 597 KB to 184 KB, and the build fails if any page still references the on-demand image route.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Content as a snapshot</h4><p>There is no database. A script pulls the blog and reports from WordPress into files committed to the repository, so every deploy is reproducible and the live site keeps working if WordPress is down. A GitHub workflow refreshes the snapshot when the team asks for it.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Guardrails for editors</h4><p>Marketing editors work through Claude Code with eleven commands such as /start, /publish and /undo. Publishing runs five checks, including a design lint that reports problems in plain English, and stops at the first failure. Passing work goes to a password-gated staging site, and production needs my approval.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

